"bm_george|Two House members voted against bills they had cosponsored. Both said they had relied on summaries of the bills written by their AI aides.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|congressional aide. A person who works for a member of Congress, reading and writing bills and counting votes.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|representative. A member of the House of Representatives, elected by the people of one district.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|district. The area, and the people in it, that a representative is elected to speak for.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|committee. A group of members of Congress who study bills on one topic before the whole House votes.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|subcommittee. A smaller group inside a committee that looks closely at one kind of issue.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|hearing. A public meeting where members of Congress ask experts and witnesses questions.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|witness. Someone invited to tell Congress what they know at a hearing.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|testify. To tell what you know in an official meeting, like a hearing or a trial.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|markup. The meeting where a committee changes a bill line by line and then votes on it.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|amendment. A change to a bill that members vote on.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|sponsor. The member of Congress who introduces a bill and leads the push to pass it.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|cosponsor. A member of Congress who signs on to support someone else\u2019s bill.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|whip count. Asking each member, one by one, how they plan to vote, before the real vote happens.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|majority. More than half.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|digital signature. A code attached to a message that only the real sender\u2019s computer can make, and that anyone can check to prove who sent the message.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|remote order. An order that reaches a robot over the internet, from an app, a company or an alert, instead of from someone standing in the room.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|security chip. A small chip inside a robot that checks the digital signature on each remote order.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|off switch. A way to stop a machine right away. A good one is easy to find and works even when the internet is down.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|regulator. A government office that writes and enforces safety rules for an industry.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|near miss. Something that almost caused an accident but didn\u2019t. Studying near misses helps people fix a problem before it causes a real accident.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|phase in. To start a rule in steps over time instead of all at once.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|enacting clause. The line in a bill that makes it law if it passes: \u201cBe it enacted by the Senate and House of Representatives.\u201d":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|definitions. The part of a law that says exactly what its words mean.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|shall. In a law, \u201cshall\u201d means must.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|may. In a law, \u201cmay\u201d means is allowed to, but does not have to.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|hopper. A wooden box beside the clerk\u2019s desk in the House, where members drop new bills.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|introduce. To formally hand in a new bill so Congress can consider it.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|veto. When the President rejects a bill and sends it back to Congress, so it does not become law unless Congress overrides the veto.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|override. To overrule a veto. It takes two thirds of the House and two thirds of the Senate.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|roll call. A vote where each member\u2019s name is called out and their vote is written down.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|loophole. A gap in a rule that lets someone get around it.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|chamber. One of the two parts of Congress: the House or the Senate.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|yield. In Congress, to give up the rest of your turn to speak.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|compromise. A deal where each side gets part of what it wants.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|tracking chip. A small chip inside a robot that reports where it is.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|permit. Official permission to make something. In 2040, factories need a permit for every new robot and computer chip they make, and only so many permits are sold each year, so new chips can be on back order.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|datacenter. A building full of computers. Most robots\u2019 AI \u201cbrains\u201d run in one, not inside the robot.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|auditor. Someone whose job is to check that rules are being followed.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
